(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["cv-introduction"]="<h1>Introduction to the Heart and Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-do-we-need-a-circulatory-system\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> Why do we need a circulatory system?</h2>\n<p>Every cell in the body needs a steady supply of oxygen and nutrients. At the same time it keeps making waste, including carbon dioxide, which must be carried away.</p>\n<p><strong>Why can't cells simply rely on diffusion?</strong> Over very short distances, diffusion is fast: oxygen crosses the few micrometres from a capillary to a nearby cell in a fraction of a second. But the time diffusion takes grows with the <strong>square</strong> of the distance. Moving oxygen 1 mm by diffusion alone takes minutes, and 1 cm takes hours.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Distance</th>\n  <th>Rough time for oxygen to diffuse</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>10 µm (capillary to a nearby cell)</td>\n  <td>about 0.02 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>1 mm</td>\n  <td>a few minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>1 cm</td>\n  <td>several hours</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A body many centimetres thick could never be supplied that way. So the body uses <strong>bulk flow</strong> instead. The heart pumps blood quickly through vessels to within a few micrometres of almost every cell, and diffusion only has to cover that last tiny step. The lecture sums up the heart's job simply: to move the blood about, sending it to all the tissues.</p>\n<p>The <strong>circulatory system</strong> has three parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Heart:</strong> the pump.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood vessels:</strong> the pipes that carry blood to every tissue and back.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood:</strong> the carrier. It transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, waste products and heat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When a tissue's needs rise, the circulation responds. Working muscle during exercise receives more blood, which brings extra oxygen and removes extra carbon dioxide.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Diffusion times are approximate (time ≈ distance² ÷ 2D, with D for oxygen in water). They are foundation material added to explain why a pump is needed. They are not on the lecture slides.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> The two major circulatory pathways</h2>\n<p>Blood travels through two circuits, one after the other:</p>\n<p><strong>Pulmonary circulation:</strong> The right side of the heart pumps blood to the lungs. There, carbon dioxide leaves the blood and oxygen enters it. The oxygen-rich blood then returns to the left side of the heart.</p>\n<p><strong>Systemic circulation:</strong> The left side of the heart pumps oxygen-rich blood to the tissues of the whole body. The tissues take up oxygen and add carbon dioxide, and the blood returns to the right side of the heart.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs01-figure-01\">\n  <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Open full-size pulmonary and systemic circulation diagram\">\n    <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" alt=\"Diagram of systemic and pulmonary circulation, showing blood travelling between the right heart and lungs, then the left heart and body organs.\" />\n  </a>\n  <figcaption><strong>Figure 1 — Pulmonary and systemic circulation.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">OpenStax College, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">original illustration</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a>; unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>The two circuits are in series.</strong> Blood must pass through one circuit before it can enter the other: body → right heart → lungs → left heart → body. There is no other route.</p>\n<p><strong>Why must both ventricles pump the same amount?</strong> Because every drop the right ventricle sends through the lungs arrives at the left ventricle. Over any period longer than a few beats, the right and left ventricles must pump the same volume per minute: about 5 L/min each at rest. If the right ventricle pumped even slightly more than the left for a long time, blood would pile up in the lungs. If the left pumped more than the right, the lungs would slowly drain. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=1-what-is-cardiac-output-and-why-does-it-matter\">→ Cardiac Output, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Within the systemic circuit, the organs sit side by side (in parallel).</strong> The aorta divides into branches that supply the brain, the heart muscle, the gut and liver, the kidneys, the skeletal muscles and the skin. Because each organ has its own branch:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>every organ receives fresh, oxygen-rich blood from the arteries, not blood another organ has already used;</li>\n<li>the flow to each organ can be turned up or down on its own, by narrowing or widening that organ's small arteries, without starving the others. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=8-why-are-arterioles-the-main-controllable-resistance-vessels\">→ Haemodynamics, §8</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lungs are the exception: all of the blood passes through them, in series.</p>\n<p><strong>The heart feeds itself the same way.</strong> The heart does not take its oxygen from the blood inside its chambers. Its muscular wall is far too thick for oxygen to diffuse in from the chambers to most of its cells. So the heart muscle has its own parallel branch from the aorta: the <strong><span data-term=\"coronary-arteries\">coronary arteries</span></strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/coronary?section=1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-separate-blood-supply\">→ Coronary Circulation, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"circuit-map\"><figcaption>Series and parallel</figcaption><p>One loop runs right heart → lungs → left heart → aorta, labelled in series. The aorta then splits into side-by-side branches for the brain, the heart muscle (coronary arteries), the gut and liver, the kidneys, skeletal muscle and the skin, labelled in parallel. The branches rejoin in the veins and return to the right heart. Gut and liver are drawn as one branch because blood from the gut passes through the liver.</p></figure>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture gives about 5.5 L/min for each side of the heart; 5 L/min is also widely quoted. The parallel arrangement of organs is foundation material for the regional-circulation chapters.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-the-heart-and-its-four-chambers\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> The heart and its four chambers</h2>\n<p>The heart is a muscular organ that works as <strong>two pumps side by side</strong>. The right heart pumps blood to the lungs, and the left heart pumps blood to the body. Each pump has two chambers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right atrium:</strong> receives blood returning from the body through the superior and inferior venae cavae.</li>\n<li><strong>Right ventricle:</strong> pumps that blood into the pulmonary trunk, which divides into the pulmonary arteries to the lungs.</li>\n<li><strong>Left atrium:</strong> receives oxygen-rich blood returning from the lungs through the pulmonary veins.</li>\n<li><strong>Left ventricle:</strong> pumps that blood into the aorta, which carries it to the whole body.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>atria</strong> mainly receive blood and top up the ventricles. The <strong>ventricles</strong> do the main pumping: they generate the pressure that drives blood into the arteries.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs01-figure-02\">\n  <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Open full-size labelled heart anatomy diagram\">\n    <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" alt=\"Sectional anatomy of the human heart showing four chambers, cardiac valves, great vessels, interventricular septum, and the thicker left ventricular wall.\" />\n  </a>\n  <figcaption><strong>Figure 2 — Internal structure of the heart.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Blausen Medical Communications, Inc., <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">original illustration</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a>; unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>How does blood flow through the heart?</strong> Follow the path:</p>\n<p><strong>Body tissues → Venae cavae → Right atrium → Tricuspid valve → Right ventricle → Pulmonary valve → Pulmonary trunk and arteries → Lungs → Pulmonary veins → Left atrium → Mitral valve → Left ventricle → Aortic valve → Aorta → Body tissues.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What keeps blood moving one way?</strong> Four valves:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>inlet valves</strong> sit between each atrium and its ventricle: the <strong>tricuspid</strong> valve on the right and the <strong>mitral</strong> valve on the left. Together they are the <span data-term=\"av-valves\">AV (atrioventricular) valves</span>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>outlet valves</strong> sit between each ventricle and its artery: the <strong>pulmonary</strong> valve on the right and the <strong>aortic</strong> valve on the left. Together they are the <span data-term=\"semilunar-valves\">semilunar valves</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Valves contain no muscle. They open and shut <strong>passively</strong>, pushed by blood pressure. A valve opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front of it, and shuts the moment that reverses. So blood can only move forward. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §2</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Arteries and veins are named by direction, not by oxygen.</strong> The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood, and the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood. This is because <strong>arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins carry blood towards it</strong>, whatever its oxygen content.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"4\" data-part=\"Sections 1–3\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–3 · 4 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why can't diffusion alone supply the cells of the body?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Diffusion stops working once blood starts to flow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Oxygen cannot cross cell membranes without a pump</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Cells use oxygen faster than any blood flow can bring it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Diffusion time rises steeply as the distance grows</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cv-introduction:99a14c47\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Diffusion time grows with the square of the distance. Micrometres take a fraction of a second, but centimetres take hours. The circulation carries blood close to every cell, so diffusion only covers the last few micrometres.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> For a week, a person's right ventricle pumps slightly more blood per minute than the left. Where does the extra blood collect?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. In the systemic veins, which hold most of the blood volume</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. In the pulmonary vessels, between the two ventricles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. In the right atrium, because the tricuspid valve shuts</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. In the aorta, which stretches to store the extra volume</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cv-introduction:b82f34a7\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The circuits are in series. Everything the right ventricle pumps goes through the lungs to the left heart. If the left ventricle sends on less than arrives, the difference builds up in the lung vessels.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why does the brain still receive oxygen-rich blood when the leg muscles are working hard?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Blood reaches the brain before it reaches the leg muscles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The brain extracts oxygen faster than the leg muscles can</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Each organ has its own branch from the aorta, side by side</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The heart pumps a separate circuit just for the brain</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cv-introduction:fc20c5fb\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Systemic organs are supplied in parallel. The brain's branch carries fresh arterial blood, not blood the muscles have already used. Flow to each organ can also be adjusted separately.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> A vessel carries oxygen-rich blood towards the heart. What is it?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. A pulmonary vein, because it carries blood towards the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A pulmonary artery, because its blood is rich in oxygen</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A systemic artery, because oxygen-rich blood leaves the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A coronary artery, because it supplies the heart muscle</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cv-introduction:def9d83e\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Vessels are named by direction. Veins carry blood towards the heart, and the pulmonary veins bring oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"4-why-is-the-left-ventricular-wall-thicker-than-the-right\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right?</h2>\n<p>Both ventricles pump the same volume each minute (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cv-introduction?section=2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways\">§2</a>). Yet the left ventricle's wall is two to three times thicker than the right's. The difference is <strong>pressure, not volume</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>What sets the pressure a pump must make?</strong> Blood flows only when there is a pressure difference to push it. The more the vessels oppose flow (their <strong><span data-term=\"resistance\">resistance</span></strong>), the bigger the pressure difference needed to keep the same flow. The lecture puts it as: flow = pressure difference ÷ resistance. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=2-what-actually-determines-flow\">→ Haemodynamics, §2</a>)</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Systemic circulation:</strong> a vast network of small, muscular arteries and arterioles, so total resistance is high. The left ventricle must reach about <strong>120 mmHg</strong> with each beat.</li>\n<li><strong>Pulmonary circulation:</strong> short, wide, thin-walled vessels, so resistance is low. The right ventricle needs only about <strong>25 mmHg</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Generating more pressure takes more muscle, so the left ventricular wall is thicker. <strong>Same volume per minute, different pressure.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why is it useful for the lungs to have low pressure?</strong> Lung capillaries are extremely thin, so that gases can cross them quickly. High pressure inside them would push fluid out into the lung tissue and air spaces. This is <strong>pulmonary oedema</strong>, and it gets in the way of gas exchange. Low pulmonary pressure keeps the lungs dry. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/microcirculation?section=5-why-does-fluid-leave-capillaries-even-when-protein-stays-in-blood\">→ Microcirculation, §5</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"5-systole-and-diastole\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Systole and diastole</h2>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"systole-diastole\">Systole</span></strong> means contraction and <strong>diastole</strong> means relaxation. Always say which chambers you mean, because the atria and ventricles contract at different times.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Atrial systole:</strong> the atria contract and push the final portion of blood into the ventricles.</li>\n<li><strong>Atrial diastole:</strong> the atria relax and fill with blood returning from the body and lungs.</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular systole:</strong> the ventricles contract and eject blood into the pulmonary trunk (right) and the aorta (left).</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular diastole:</strong> the ventricles relax and fill for the next beat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The order matters.</strong> The atria contract <strong>at the end of ventricular diastole</strong>, just before the ventricles contract. By then, most ventricular filling (about 70% in the lecture) has already happened passively. Atrial contraction only tops it up.</p>\n<p>At a resting heart rate, one beat lasts about 0.8 s. The ventricles spend about 0.3 s in systole and 0.5 s in diastole. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=1-one-heartbeat-what-the-atria-and-ventricles-are-doing\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"6-cardiac-muscle-and-the-conducting-system\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> Cardiac muscle and the conducting system</h2>\n<p>To pump well, the heart needs two things: <strong>force</strong> and <strong>timing</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Force: cardiac muscle (myocardium).</strong> Cardiac muscle cells are electrically linked to one another. An impulse therefore spreads from cell to cell, and the muscle of each chamber contracts as one unit. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=2-intercalated-discs-how-separate-cells-function-together\">→ Cardiac Muscle, §2</a>)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Timing: the conducting system.</strong> The impulse starts in the <strong><span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node</span></strong>, at the junction of the superior vena cava and the right atrium. It spreads across the atria to the <strong><span data-term=\"av-node\">AV node</span></strong>, which holds it back briefly. It then races down the <strong>bundle of His</strong> and the <strong><span data-term=\"his-purkinje\">Purkinje fibres</span></strong> through both ventricles. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=2-the-pathway-of-cardiac-electrical-conduction\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §2</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture notes that every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. The SA node normally sets the pace because it fires <strong>fastest</strong>, so it triggers the rest before they can fire themselves. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Electrical activation always comes first; contraction follows.</strong> The order in which the impulse spreads sets the order of contraction: the atria first, then, after the AV-node delay, the ventricles.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Lecture connection: Circulation 1, opening slides (the heart's structure and function, conducting tissues, systole and diastole). The diffusion explanation and the parallel arrangement of organs are added foundations.</aside>\n\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"4\" data-part=\"Sections 4–6\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 4–6 · 4 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Both ventricles eject about 70 mL per beat. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It must eject a larger volume of blood each minute</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It must generate higher pressure against higher resistance</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It contracts for longer because the aortic valve is smaller</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It must also pump blood through the coronary arteries first</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cv-introduction:da13cece\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The two ventricles move the same volume, but the systemic circulation offers far more resistance. The left ventricle needs about 120 mmHg against the right's 25, and more pressure needs more muscle.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why is low pressure in the pulmonary circulation an advantage?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It lets the right ventricle pump less blood than the left</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It slows the blood so that more carbon dioxide can be made</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It stops the pulmonary valve from opening during systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It keeps fluid from being pushed out of the lung capillaries</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cv-introduction:6d9c6101\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Thin lung capillaries leak fluid if pressure inside them is high, which causes pulmonary oedema. Low pressure keeps the lungs dry. Both ventricles still pump the same volume.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> During atrial systole, what are the ventricles normally doing?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Contracting, so the atria and ventricles squeeze together</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Ejecting blood into the aorta and pulmonary trunk at once</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Relaxed, finishing filling just before their own contraction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Relaxing after ejection, with all four valves still shut</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cv-introduction:81add81f\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. They top up the relaxed ventricles just before the ventricles contract; the AV-node delay keeps that order.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. Why does the SA node normally set the heart rate?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It fires fastest, so it triggers the others first</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It is the only part connected to the nervous system</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It lies in the left ventricle, the strongest chamber</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It holds the impulse back so the atria contract first</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cv-introduction:43726271\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The fastest pacemaker wins: the SA node fires before the others reach their own firing point. D describes the AV node. The SA node lies in the right atrium.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"7-understanding-check\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> Understanding Check</h2><section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\">\n<p><strong>1.</strong> For several minutes, a patient's left ventricle pumps less than the right ventricle. Where does blood build up first?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. In the lungs, raising the pressure in the pulmonary vessels</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. In the leg veins, causing swelling around the ankles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. In the right ventricle, whose wall thickens to cope</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. In the pulmonary veins, which then carry oxygen-poor blood</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cv-introduction:3d341f02\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The lungs sit between the right and left ventricles. If the left side sends on less than the right side delivers, blood accumulates in the lung vessels first.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Which statement about the heart valves is correct?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Valves open when their muscle fibres contract</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Valves open and shut because of pressure differences</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Valves are opened by impulses from the AV node</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Valves shut as soon as the atria begin to relax</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cv-introduction:7f9b9fda\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Valves have no muscle and no nerve control. Pressure behind a valve opens it, and reversed pressure shuts it.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> The right ventricle pumps oxygen-poor blood into the pulmonary trunk. Why is the pulmonary trunk still called an artery?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It has a thick muscular wall like the aorta</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It carries blood at the highest pressure in the body</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It carries blood away from the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It carries blood back to the left atrium</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cv-introduction:8c928c5b\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins carry it towards the heart, whatever the oxygen content.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"8-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">8.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Diffusion only works over micrometres.</strong> The circulation brings blood within a few micrometres of almost every cell.</li>\n<li><strong>The pulmonary and systemic circuits are in series.</strong> Both ventricles pump the same volume per minute (about 5 L/min at rest). A lasting mismatch makes blood pile up in one circuit.</li>\n<li><strong>The systemic organs are in parallel.</strong> Each gets fresh arterial blood, and flow to each can be adjusted separately. The heart muscle has its own branch: the coronary arteries.</li>\n<li><strong>Four chambers:</strong> the atria receive blood and top up the ventricles; the ventricles generate the pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Valves are passive.</strong> They open and shut with pressure differences, keeping flow one-way.</li>\n<li><strong>Arteries carry blood away from the heart; veins carry it towards the heart</strong>, whatever its oxygen content.</li>\n<li><strong>The left ventricle is thicker because it needs more pressure</strong> (about 120 vs 25 mmHg), not because it pumps more volume. Low lung pressure keeps the lungs dry.</li>\n<li><strong>Systole is contraction; diastole is relaxation.</strong> The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. Electrical activation (SA node → AV node → His–Purkinje) comes first and sets the order of contraction.</li>\n</ol>\n";
